<Layout title="Technical SEO & Website Migrations" description="Technical SEO audits and website migration support from Shahzad Akram. Clear priorities, developer coordination and planning from discovery through launch.">
<article class="wrapper marketing">
<section class="hero">
<p class="eyebrow">TECHNICAL SEO &amp; WEBSITE MIGRATIONS</p>
<h1>Technical SEO support for the website you have—and the one you’re planning</h1>
<p>Identify what needs fixing, give your developers clear priorities and bring SEO into important website decisions.</p>
<p>I help businesses investigate technical search problems and plan website rebuilds, platform changes and migrations. My work connects SEO requirements with the practical decisions involved in developing, launching and maintaining a website.</p>
<p class="actions"><a href="/contact/">Discuss your website or migration</a> · <a href="/work/">Explore my experience</a></p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Experience from planning through to launch</h2>
<p>I’m Shahzad Akram. I have led nine major website launches and migrations across the UK, US and Australia.</p>
<p>My responsibilities have included platform selection, domain strategy, website structure, analytics, hosting discussions and launch planning. I work with developers, agencies and internal teams from initial requirements through to post-launch improvements.</p>
<p>That experience spans WordPress, Shopify, Squarespace and custom website environments, including websites serving franchise networks and international markets.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>When technical SEO support is useful</h2>
<h3>Your website’s performance has changed</h3>
<p>Organic traffic or enquiries have declined, important pages are struggling to appear in search, or recent website changes have introduced uncertainty.</p>
<p>I help investigate the evidence, distinguish technical issues from other possible causes and identify what to address first.</p>
<h3>You have an audit but need a practical plan</h3>
<p>A long list of issues is difficult to act on without context.</p>
<p>I can review existing findings, investigate the important ones and turn them into priorities with clear implementation requirements.</p>
<h3>You are rebuilding or changing platforms</h3>
<p>A redesign can affect URLs, content, navigation, tracking and how pages are delivered.</p>
<p>I help bring SEO into the project while those decisions are being made, so requirements are understood before launch.</p>
<h3>Your website has become difficult to manage</h3>
<p>Growing service lists, location pages and years of content can leave a website with overlapping pages and confusing navigation.</p>
<p>I review how the site is organised and recommend a structure that supports customers, search discovery and ongoing maintenance.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Technical SEO audits</h2>
<h3>Understand what search engines can access</h3>
<p>Review how important pages are discovered and whether technical controls create unintended barriers.</p>
<p>The scope may include crawling, indexing directives, robots.txt, XML sitemaps, canonical tags and the way content is rendered.</p>
<h3>Review website structure and internal links</h3>
<p>Assess how services, locations, categories and supporting content connect.</p>
<p>I look for important pages that are difficult to reach, unnecessary overlap and opportunities to make navigation more useful.</p>
<h3>Investigate duplicate and competing pages</h3>
<p>Identify where similar URLs or content create confusion.</p>
<p>Depending on the website, this may involve filtered pages, URL parameters, repeated location content, legacy URLs or several pages serving the same purpose.</p>
<h3>Check templates and on-page implementation</h3>
<p>Review the technical elements shared across page types, including titles, headings, metadata and relevant structured data.</p>
<p>Template problems can affect many pages at once, so understanding their scope helps prioritise the work.</p>
<h3>Assess performance and usability issues</h3>
<p>Investigate speed and layout problems that affect the experience of using the website.</p>
<p>Where specialist development work is needed, I help define the issue and coordinate the next steps with your technical team.</p>
<h3>Review measurement</h3>
<p>Check that the website changes being assessed can be measured meaningfully.</p>
<p>This may include analytics implementation, key enquiry events and consistency between reporting periods. More detailed tracking investigations can be scoped separately.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>What you receive</h2>
<p>An audit should make the next decisions easier.</p>
<p>Depending on the agreed scope, the output includes:</p>
<ul><li>A summary of the main findings and their likely significance.</li><li>Examples showing where each issue occurs.</li><li>Priorities based on impact, scale and implementation effort.</li><li>Recommended actions and relevant dependencies.</li><li>Clear briefs for developers or content teams.</li><li>A review of completed changes where included in the engagement.</li></ul>
<p>I explain where the evidence is conclusive, where further investigation is needed and which items are improvements rather than urgent faults.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Website migration support</h2>
<h3>Before development</h3>
<p>Establish what the current website does well and what the new website needs to retain.</p>
<p>I review important pages, existing search performance, website structure and the proposed changes. This helps bring SEO requirements into the project brief.</p>
<h3>During planning and development</h3>
<p>Work with the delivery team on the details that affect search performance.</p>
<p>Depending on the project, these may include:</p>
<ul><li>New page structure and navigation.</li><li>URL conventions.</li><li>Content retention and consolidation.</li><li>Redirect mapping.</li><li>Canonical and indexing controls.</li><li>International or location-specific requirements.</li><li>Analytics and enquiry tracking.</li><li>XML sitemaps and internal links.</li></ul>
<h3>Before launch</h3>
<p>Review the staging website against the agreed requirements.</p>
<p>The aim is to identify problems while they can still be addressed within the launch plan, and to establish who owns the remaining actions.</p>
<h3>At launch</h3>
<p>Check the elements most likely to affect access, navigation and measurement.</p>
<p>This includes reviewing redirects, important pages, indexing controls, internal links and tracking, with the exact checks determined by the migration.</p>
<h3>After launch</h3>
<p>Monitor the website and investigate issues as they emerge.</p>
<p>I review available crawl, search and analytics data, coordinate fixes and help distinguish expected changes from problems requiring attention.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Migration experience across different website needs</h2>
<p>My launch and migration experience includes UK, US and Australian websites, alongside responsibility for search performance across multiple brands.</p>
<p>These projects involve decisions beyond transferring pages. They require coordination around content, platforms, domains, hosting, analytics and the teams maintaining the website after launch.</p>
<p>I bring that wider perspective to the SEO work, helping make requirements clear and keeping them connected to the project’s commercial purpose.</p>
<p><a href="/work/">Explore my work</a></p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Working with your developers and agency</h2>
<p>I can work alongside your existing delivery team.</p>
<p>We agree who is responsible for each part of the project, how recommendations will be documented and when changes should be reviewed.</p>
<p>My role can include:</p>
<ul><li>Contributing SEO requirements to the project brief.</li><li>Reviewing proposed structures and templates.</li><li>Preparing implementation tasks.</li><li>Discussing trade-offs with developers.</li><li>Checking completed changes.</li><li>Reporting outstanding issues and decisions.</li></ul>
<p>Development remains with the people best placed to carry it out. I help ensure the SEO requirements are understood and checked.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>How we would get started</h2>
<h3>1. Establish the scope</h3>
<p>We discuss the website, the problem or planned change, your objectives and any fixed deadlines.</p>
<h3>2. Review the available evidence</h3>
<p>I assess the relevant website behaviour, search data, analytics and project documentation.</p>
<h3>3. Agree priorities and responsibilities</h3>
<p>We identify what needs investigating or implementing, who will deliver it and what depends on other work.</p>
<h3>4. Support delivery</h3>
<p>I prepare recommendations or briefs, work with the relevant team and review implementation within the agreed scope.</p>
<h3>5. Check the outcome</h3>
<p>We confirm what changed, assess the available evidence and identify any remaining work.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can you review an audit another agency has produced?</h3>
<p>Yes. I can assess the findings, investigate the priorities and help turn the recommendations into a practical delivery plan.</p>
<h3>Can you implement technical fixes yourself?</h3>
<p>That depends on the platform, access and complexity of the changes. I can make some updates directly and coordinate others with developers. We would agree responsibilities before starting.</p>
<h3>When should SEO become involved in a rebuild?</h3>
<p>Ideally, while the brief, website structure and platform decisions are being developed. That gives the team time to account for existing content, URLs and search requirements before implementation.</p>
<h3>Can you help if the migration has already happened?</h3>
<p>Yes. I can review the live website, compare available pre-launch and post-launch information and investigate issues that may have been introduced.</p>
<h3>Can you guarantee that traffic will stay the same after a migration?</h3>
<p>No. A migration can change how pages are accessed, understood and ranked. My role is to identify risks, plan the relevant work, check implementation and investigate performance after launch.</p>
<h3>Do you work with franchise and multi-location websites?</h3>
<p>Yes. My experience includes websites with substantial physical-location and service-area content. I can assess the relationship between national services, local pages and franchise recruitment platforms.</p>
<h3>Can you review an international website launch?</h3>
<p>Yes. My experience includes launches across the UK, US and Australia. The scope would depend on the markets, languages, domain structure and platform involved.</p>
<h3>How is the work priced?</h3>
<p>I would first establish the size of the website, the scope, the available access and the support required. I can then propose a defined project or ongoing arrangement with clear deliverables.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Let’s discuss your website</h2>
<p>Tell me what is changing, what is causing concern and whether you are working towards a launch date.</p>
<p>I can help establish the technical SEO priorities and how they fit into the wider project.</p>
<p><a href="/contact/">Discuss your audit or migration</a></p>
<p>Or email <a href="mailto:contact@shahzadakram.com">contact@shahzadakram.com</a>.</p>
</section>
</article>
